557123 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186556668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557123 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "640612ec-cca7-54d8-80ce-3588c7e329a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557216 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557216 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95938" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557216 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557216 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{2}-3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}-2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557216 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "220c02c6-9021-54d4-b05d-5f454f3a5b70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557216 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{2}-3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}-2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=18{q}_{1}+6{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=5{q}_{1}+10{q}_{2}+5{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=4+5+5=14.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557255 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557255 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140899" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557255 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557255 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557255 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6466be13-c99b-575a-b71f-807fcb74c35a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557255 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557282 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557282 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95925" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557282 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557282 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557282 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91f32261-8a78-5d95-90b2-1f1ea2788664" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557282 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
